{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkT5b81RdiUfed2mU4uC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The repaired candidate passes the self-check. I couldn't test whether it now finishes within the 30-minute admission limit, because the check doesn't time the full fit. The smoke check said `OK`, and its ELPD-LOO was −3605.4 on a short 100-draw fit.\n\n**What changed:** last time's model was the current best model (`gamblers_second_order_person_lapse`) with one addition: a new generator people might suspect, a \"coin swap\" partway through the sequence. It also gave every person their own weight for that generator, which meant another 400-slot population of parameters. That population was the only difference from the best model, which does fit in time. Each person's swap weight was probably barely pinned down by the data and traded off against their own motif-suspicion weight. That kind of shape makes the sampler take its longest possible steps, which is why it ran out of time.\n\n**The fix:** the swap weight is now one shared parameter (`c_swap`, Normal(0, 1) on the log scale) instead of a per-person population. Everything else stays the same. The likelihood was already computed for all trials at once, and the swap evidence was already worked out once per distinct sequence.\n\n**The hypothesis:** besides their own (gambler's-fallacy) picture of a fair coin, people also suspect that the coin was switched partway through: one rigged coin for the first stretch, another for the rest. So sequences like HHHHHTTT, HHTHHTTT or THTTTHHH look staged even when their switch count and heads/tails balance are ordinary. I updated `hypothesis.md` and the module docstring to say the swap weight is shared. The model name stays `coin_swap_changepoint_suspicion`.\n\n**Still open:**\n- **Novelty:** the check doesn't test whether the model predicts differently enough from the existing ones. With one shared swap weight it is closer to the best model than before, so admission could reject it as a near-duplicate.\n- **Convergence:** a single chain can't report R-hat, so convergence wasn't verified.\n- **LOO reliability:** the smoke fit flagged PSIS-LOO as unreliable (6.8% of trials had high Pareto k). That may be partly because the fit was so short.\n\nAll three files are in `experiment3/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_2_repair_1/`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2939,"cache_read_input_tokens":83008,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2939,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56229923-6388-41f2-9271-17b52ee94a5d","uuid":"4042ca53-e584-40da-ba01-83d06d3d33d9","timestamp":"2026-10-06T05:43:09.497Z","request_id":"req_011CfkT5akguuEPfRzzrbzmD"}
